# Everything Memory — engine configuration (self-host / build-from-source)
#
# Copy to local.env and fill in your values:
#   cp local.env.template local.env
#   ln -s local.env .env     # compose reads .env for its `${VAR:?}` checks
#
# local.env is git-ignored — never commit real keys.
# Every value below is REQUIRED unless marked optional (the engine has no defaults).

# -----------------------------------
# PostgreSQL credentials (required)
# Must match across all services in docker-compose.yml.
# -----------------------------------
# The password has no default anywhere: `docker compose up` refuses without
# it. Mint one (openssl rand -hex 24). It is fixed into the database volume
# the first time the stack starts.
POSTGRES_DB=ememory
POSTGRES_USER=ememory
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# -----------------------------------
# Where the memory lives on this machine (required)
# -----------------------------------
# ARCHIVE_DIR — the host folder you name for the archive: the durable,
# ENCRYPTED copy of everything (the stack's own archive store writes here).
# Required, no default; an absolute path with no . or .. in it. It is a bind
# mount, so `docker compose down -v` never touches it; back it up and move it
# with your own tools, on any disk. Make it BEFORE the first start (a missing
# folder is refused, never created):
#   mkdir -p /srv/ememory/archive
# On macOS (Docker Desktop) put it under a folder Docker shares — /Users,
# /Volumes or /private by default — e.g. /Users/<you>/ememory/archive.
# Its key, ARCHIVE_KEY (below), is kept APART from it.
ARCHIVE_DIR=

# -----------------------------------
# The job queue's password (required)
# -----------------------------------
# Redis, the engine's job queue, answers nothing without it; the engine and
# every worker carry it. No default anywhere: `docker compose up` refuses
# without it. Mint one (hex, so it needs no quoting):
#   openssl rand -hex 24
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# -----------------------------------
# Ports and the stack's network (optional)
# -----------------------------------
# The engine's and the console's host ports (both on 127.0.0.1 only), and the
# name of the stack's Docker network. Defaults: 8765, 8766, `ememory`. Change
# them only to run a second stack on this machine — together with its own
# project name: `docker compose -p <name> up -d`. Images built with
# docker-compose.build.yml keep fixed tags shared by every stack here: never
# `--build` a different checkout beside a live stack.
#EMEMORY_PORT=8765
#CONSOLE_PORT=8766
#EMEMORY_NETWORK=ememory

# -----------------------------------
# Console sign-in sessions (optional)
# -----------------------------------
# How long a person stays signed in to the console: 30m, 12h, 30d, or 0 for
# no limit. IDLE signs out after this long unused (default 0: never); MAX
# signs out this long after sign-in, however busy (default 30d). Empty or
# unset = the default; a malformed value stops the console at start.
#CONSOLE_SESSION_IDLE=0
#CONSOLE_SESSION_MAX=30d

# -----------------------------------
# Authentication (required: true or false)
# -----------------------------------
# false is for trying eMemory on THIS machine: the engine then accepts every
# caller with no key, and the compose publishes its port on 127.0.0.1 only, so
# nothing off this machine can reach it.
#
# To reach the engine from other machines:
#   1. set AUTH_ENABLED=true;
#   2. add at least one key hash to API_KEY_HASH_LIST below;
#   3. add the overlay that publishes 8765 on every interface (it forces auth
#      on, so the engine refuses to start without a key or OAuth):
#        COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.open.yml
#      (with the estate or Qdrant overlays as well, list them all in one line).
AUTH_ENABLED=false

# -----------------------------------
# Client keys (one or more when AUTH_ENABLED=true)
# -----------------------------------
# With auth on, every caller but /health sends `Authorization: Bearer <key>`;
# the engine holds only each key's SHA-256, and refuses to start with auth on
# and neither a key here nor OAuth (OAUTH_ISSUER) configured.
#
# Give each harness its own key (each agent host, e.sh, a service beside the
# engine, and the console - a dedicated key for people): every key is equal,
# and removing a hash revokes that one key alone. Mint a pair per holder (the
# key starts emem_):
#   ./e.sh client-key mint
# Give the key to that harness (MCP header Authorization: Bearer <key>; for
# e.sh, --token or EMEMORY_API_KEY). The key is printed once — keep it, or mint
# a new pair. Put the hashes here, comma-separated:
#   API_KEY_HASH_LIST=<hash of key 1>,<hash of key 2>
API_KEY_HASH_LIST=

# -----------------------------------
# Embeddings (required)
# -----------------------------------
# The engine's ONLY model call: an OpenAI-compatible /v1/embeddings endpoint.
# The stack ships one — the `gateway` service (LiteLLM), configured by
# conf/gateway.yaml — and, behind it, a local model: nomic-embed-text v1.5 in
# the `local-embed` service. As shipped no key is needed and nothing leaves
# this machine. The engine carries no vendor SDK.
#
# TO SWITCH TO OPENROUTER: set OPENROUTER_API_KEY in gateway.env (from
# gateway.env.template) and EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small. A new
# model is a new embedding space: the embedding sweep re-embeds the whole
# corpus into it.
#
# BOTH ARE REQUIRED AND HAVE NO DEFAULT ANYWHERE: the app and every worker
# refuse to start, naming the variable, when either is missing or blank, and
# `docker compose up` refuses first.
#   EMBEDDING_BASE_URL  the endpoint, incl. /v1
#   EMBEDDING_MODEL     the route conf/gateway.yaml names
EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=http://gateway:4000/v1
EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# The gateway's provider keys are NOT here: they live in gateway.env (from
# gateway.env.template), which only the gateway reads.
# Bearer key for that endpoint (optional — leave empty when it needs none).
EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
# Changing EMBEDDING_MODEL is a CUT-OVER: every SR is "missing" in the new
# space, and the next hourly embedding sweep queues the whole corpus at once,
# in batches, on the maintenance queue, where the maintenance worker embeds
# them. The batch size is `embed_batch` in conf/limits.yaml.
# The tag index has a sweep of its own. Every hour, and at startup, it counts
# the records with no current tag row and queues every one of them, in batches,
# on the maintenance queue, where the maintenance worker puts them through the
# plugins. The batch size is `tag_derive_batch` in conf/limits.yaml; the
# maintenance worker's concurrency is MAINTENANCE_WORKER_MAX_JOBS.
#MAINTENANCE_WORKER_MAX_JOBS=5

# -----------------------------------
# Pipeline profile (required)
# -----------------------------------
# The shipped stack reads time with chrono: `retrieval_v1`
# extracts it at ingest through plugin_chrono, and `timeline_v1` reads the
# e_timeline question with it. Both run on the compose as shipped; the
# Duckling profiles (`agentic_eval_v2`, `temporal`) need Duckling's services,
# which the shipped compose does not carry.
PIPELINE_PROFILE=retrieval_v1
# The profiles.yaml block e_timeline runs. An unknown name refuses the boot.
TEMPORAL_PROFILE=timeline_v1

# -----------------------------------
# Worker cron jobs (optional — default off)
# -----------------------------------
# On: the sweeps — including the one that restores the database from the
# archive — run on the workers' schedule.
CRON_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------
# The archive (required — on by default)
# -----------------------------------
# The archive is the DURABLE store: every record, correction, update, policy
# and vector is written to it as an object. Postgres is the live index, and is
# rebuilt from the archive when it is lost (the sync sweep, at every worker
# start and every five minutes). As shipped the archive is the stack's own
# `archive-store` service (SeaweedFS) on this machine's disk — no internet.
#
# TO MOVE IT to another S3-compatible store (AWS S3, MinIO, …): point S3_ENDPOINT_URL,
# S3_REGION, S3_BUCKET and the key pair at that store (the bucket must exist).
#
# KEEP ARCHIVE_KEY APART FROM THE ARCHIVE — in a password manager.
# Without it the archive cannot be read; beside it, the encryption protects
# nothing.
ARCHIVE_ENABLED=true
S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://archive-store:8333
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=ememory-archive
# Optional: a prefix inside the bucket, for a bucket several engines share.
S3_KEY_PREFIX=
# The store's key pair — required, no default (the stack's archive-bucket step
# refuses to finish without it, and the engine waits for that step). On the
# shipped store, any pair
# you mint becomes its one identity at start:
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(16))"   # access key
#   python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"   # secret key
S3_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_SECRET_KEY=
# The key every archive object is encrypted with (AES-256-GCM) — required
# whenever the archive is on, no default: the engine and every worker refuse
# to start without it, by name. Mint it ONCE, keep it in a password manager,
# never beside the archive: a lost key is an unreadable archive.
#   python3 -c "import base64, os; print(base64.b64encode(os.urandom(32)).decode())"
ARCHIVE_KEY=

# -----------------------------------
# Qdrant (optional — the default stack needs nothing here)
# -----------------------------------
# WHERE THE VECTORS LIVE. pgvector (the default) keeps them in the engine's own
# Postgres: `docker compose up -d` needs no setting and no key. Qdrant keeps
# them in the `qdrant` service of `docker-compose.qdrant.yml`, and the whole
# engine routes through the seam — ingest, the embedding sweep, the 1:1 window
# pool, the peer embedding stream in both directions, archive rehydration and
# the DR census. One store per stack, chosen once; an
# unknown VECTOR_STORE fails the boot rather than answering from nowhere.
#
# To switch, layer the overlay and mint a key:
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.qdrant.yml
#   QDRANT_API_KEY=<openssl rand -hex 24>
# (or pass `-f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.qdrant.yml` per command).
# The overlay sets VECTOR_STORE=qdrant and QDRANT_URL for the app and every
# worker, and refuses to start without a non-empty key: an unkeyed vector store
# on the estate network is an open door to the corpus.
#
# SWITCHING AN EXISTING STACK DOES NOT MOVE ITS VECTORS. The new store starts
# empty and the next embedding sweep refills it from the records, the whole
# corpus queued at once — an API call per record. The old pgvector rows are left
# untouched, so switching back is the same setting again.
#COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.qdrant.yml
#QDRANT_API_KEY=

# -----------------------------------
# The shared `estate` network (optional — the default stack needs nothing here)
# -----------------------------------
# Only where other stacks on this machine reach the engine by name (a vehicle,
# a store, a stage, an embedding gateway on `estate`). The overlay joins the
# whole stack to the external `estate` network and publishes Postgres on
# 127.0.0.1:${POSTGRES_PORT:-5544} for host tooling. The network must exist
# (`docker network create estate`). With the Qdrant overlay as well, list all
# four files.
#COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.build.yml:docker-compose.estate.yml
